 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Vertex Work Increase:** Because whole cubes are submitted and hidden faces are culled in the shader, vertex work increases by 2.0× to 3.3× for zero-gap layouts (e.g., a 4×4×4 block with a 0 in the center) compared to a mesh that only generates visible faces [S1, unofficial]. This is acceptable as it is vertex-only work and avoids the cost of per-frame buffer rebuilds or multiple draw calls [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Precision Drift:** GPU `f32` arithmetic drift is bounded to under $10^{-7}$ of the unit box, which is less than half a step of an 8-bit channel, ensuring visual correctness [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Matrix Multiplication Order:** Row-major vs. column-major matrix multiplication must be handled correctly, especially when using uniforms that may be stored in row-major order [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Vertex Work Increase:** Submitting whole cubes and culling in the shader increases vertex work by 2.0× to 3.3× for zero-gap layouts (e.g., solid blocks) compared to only drawing visible faces [S1, unofficial]. This is acceptable as it is vertex-only work and avoids the cost of per-frame buffer rebuilds or multiple draw calls [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Precision Drift:** GPU float32 arithmetic drift is bounded to under 1e-7 of the unit box, which is less than half a step of an 8-bit channel, ensuring visual correctness [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Matrix Multiplication Order:** Ensure correct row-major vs. column-major handling, especially when porting between languages or using specific shader compilers (e.g., SPIRV_Cross) [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Hidden Face Culling:** To survive translucent fade-ins, hidden faces should be moved outside the clip volume rather than simply not drawn [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Geometry build speed is 1.9–4.6× faster than pre-branch core methods [S1, unofficial]. Whole build speed is 1.3–1.8× faster [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is significantly reduced. At resolution R=64, specimen memory drops from 42.0 MB to 649 KB (19–65× smaller) [S1, unofficial]. Worker message size reduces from 42.3 MB to 457 KB (92× reduction) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Cache Efficiency:** Cache capacity increases from 6 specimens to about 394 specimens before exhausting `CACHE_BYTES` at R=64 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Geometry build speed is 1.9–4.6× faster than pre-branch core methods; whole build speed is 1.3–1.8× faster [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory per voxel is reduced to 6 bytes (vs. over 1 kilobyte for six baked quads) [S1, unofficial]. At resolution R=64, specimen memory drops from 42.0 MB to 649 KB, and worker message size drops from 42.3 MB to 457 KB (92× reduction) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Cache Efficiency:** Cache capacity at R=64 increases from 6 specimens to about 394 specimens before exhausting memory limits [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Performance:** Maintains interactive frame rates of approximately 60 FPS at high voxel densities [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Low memory footprint, fast build times, single draw call, flexible visibility logic.
-*   **Limitations:** Increased vertex throughput for dense, gap-free volumes due to full cube submission.
+*   **Strengths:** Significant memory reduction, fast build times, single draw call, flexible procedural logic.
+*   **Limitations:** Increased vertex workload for dense/zero-gap layouts due to whole-cube submission [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method is used in projects such as the block-showroom by lardratboy [S1, unofficial], and is implemented in Unity via procedural instancing shaders [S2, S3, S4, unofficial] and OpenGL via SSBOs [S5, unofficial]. It evolved from baked mesh approaches to reduce memory overhead and improve build times [S1, unofficial].
+This technique is used in projects like the block-showroom by lardratboy, specifically in `src/scene/instancing.js` and `lattice/recipe.js` [S1, unofficial]. It is also implemented in Unity-based voxel renderers such as UnityGPUMeshVoxelizerPart4/Part5 and Voxel-Shader [S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. The method leverages procedural instancing features available in modern graphics APIs and engines to avoid CPU-side mesh generation for every voxel [S3, S4, unofficial].
 
